  "ref": "JOB.42.8",
  "review_notes": [
    "Correction: t001 *Now* moves to `עַתָּה` and t002 *therefore* to the waw. The adverb's own lexeme is what *now* writes, so rule 11 assigns that word to it and rule 24 confirms it as the most direct realization; the waw then takes the inferential connective, which rule 14's open list already carries. The units cross under rule 25, which the contract permits and rule 51 requires: distance from the waw, and the side of the clause the word falls on, are not evidence about which morpheme a connective belongs to.",
    "This aligns the verse with JOB.42.5, where `וְעַתָּה` → *But now* gives *But* to the waw and *now* to `עַתָּה`. Left as it stood, one construction was analysed two ways with nothing in the source licensing the difference.",
    "The competing reading takes each morpheme to hold the English word standing in its own position, so that a waw always takes the connective at the clause's left edge and `עַתָּה` takes whatever follows. It is recorded rather than graded (rule 8); the precedent supplied to this pass carries no `עַתָּה` rows, so the division rests on rules 11, 24, 25 and 51 rather than on corpus counts.",
    "`כִּי אִם` could be read as one fused unit; the ASV writes only *for*, so `כִּי` takes it and the asseverative `אִם` is unrepresented.",
    "*after* was read as the preposition the English verb *deal* needs to reach the bare object `נְבָלָה` (rule 44); the alternative marks it `added`.",
    "The closing *hath* is read as in v7."
  ]
}
